{"name":"Palladian","hex":"#eee9df","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Palladian","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to Pallas, an epithet of Athena, the goddess of wisdom, and potentially to other female Greek figures such as Pallas the daughter of Triton and granddaughter of Poseidon.","Of or relating to knowledge, study, or wisdom."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["In the style of the Italian neoclassical architect Andrea Palladio."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Palladian"}],"entries":[{"title":"Palladian architecture","blocks":[{"text":"Palladian architecture is a European architectural style derived from the work of the Venetian architect Andrea Palladio (1508–1580). What is today recognised as Palladian architecture evolved from his concepts of symmetry, perspective and the principles of formal classical architecture from ancient Greek and Roman traditions. In the 17th and 18th centuries, Palladio's interpretation of this classical architecture developed into the style known as Palladianism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Andrea_palladio_fourth_book_image.jpg/330px-Andrea_palladio_fourth_book_image.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palladian_architecture","wikidata":"Q264649"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/PalladioRotondaPlan.jpg/330px-PalladioRotondaPlan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/PalladioRotondaPlan.jpg/330px-PalladioRotondaPlan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Plan for Palladio's Villa La Rotonda (c. 1565) – features of the house were incorporated in numerous Palladian-style houses throughout Europe over the following centuries.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalladioRotondaPlan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Palladio_Villa_Godi.jpg/500px-Palladio_Villa_Godi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Palladio_Villa_Godi.jpg/1280px-Palladio_Villa_Godi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Palladio_Villa_Godi.jpg/500px-Palladio_Villa_Godi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"True Palladianism\" at the Villa Godi (1537–1542) – from Palladio's I quattro libri dell'architettura. The flanking pavilions are agricultural buildings, not part of the villa. In the 18th century, the connecting colonnades evolved as enfilades of rooms, while the pavilions often became self-contained wings or blocks – a common feature of 18th century Palladianism.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APalladio_Villa_Godi.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/08-Villa-Rotonda-Palladio.jpg/500px-08-Villa-Rotonda-Palladio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/08-Villa-Rotonda-Palladio.jpg/1280px-08-Villa-Rotonda-Palladio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/08-Villa-Rotonda-Palladio.jpg/500px-08-Villa-Rotonda-Palladio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Villa Capra \"La Rotonda\" (begun c. 1565) – one of Palladio's most influential designs","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A08-Villa-Rotonda-Palladio.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Vicenza_Basilica_Palladiana_03.jpg/500px-Vicenza_Basilica_Palladiana_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Vicenza_Basilica_Palladiana_03.jpg/1280px-Vicenza_Basilica_Palladiana_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Vicenza_Basilica_Palladiana_03.jpg/500px-Vicenza_Basilica_Palladiana_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Basilica Palladiana, Vicenza (from 1546) – loggia with Palladian windows","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVicenza_Basilica_Palladiana_03.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Claydon_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1285177.jpg/500px-Claydon_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1285177.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Claydon_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1285177.jpg/500px-Claydon_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1285177.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Claydon House (begun 1757) – the Venetian window in the central bay is surrounded by a unifying blind arch[30]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AClaydon_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1285177.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Queens_House.jpg/500px-Queens_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Queens_House.jpg/1280px-Queens_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Queens_House.jpg/500px-Queens_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Queen's House, Greenwich (begun 1616) – Inigo Jones's masterpiece[39]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AQueens_House.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Wanstead_-_intended_design.jpg/500px-Wanstead_-_intended_design.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Wanstead_-_intended_design.jpg/500px-Wanstead_-_intended_design.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wanstead House (1722) – among the first, and largest, of the Neo-Palladian houses; the image is from Colen Campbell's Vitruvius Britannicus.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWanstead_-_intended_design.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Holkham-Hall-South-Facafe.jpg/500px-Holkham-Hall-South-Facafe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Holkham-Hall-South-Facafe.jpg/1280px-Holkham-Hall-South-Facafe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Holkham-Hall-South-Facafe.jpg/500px-Holkham-Hall-South-Facafe.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Holkham Hall, South front (1734) – the four flanking wings are elevated, in height and importance, almost to the status of the central block.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHolkham-Hall-South-Facafe.jpg"}],"sounds":{"Palladian":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/En-uk-Palladian.oga?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/a3/En-uk-Palladian.oga/En-uk-Palladian.oga.mp3"}]}}}